theory TESLA_Scheme1 begin

builtins: signing, hashing

functions: f/1, bar/1, mac/2

rule Generate_key_pair:
  [
    Fr(~sk_S)
  ]
  -->
  [
    !Ltk('S', ~sk_S),
    !Pk('S', pk(~sk_S)),
    Out(pk(~sk_S))
  ]

rule LtkReveal:
  [
    !Ltk(A, ltk)
  ]
  --[ LtkReveal(A) ]->
  [ Out(ltk) ]

rule RuleStep1:
  [
    Fr(~n_R)
  ]
  --[ Test1(), Honest('R'), Commit('R', 'S', <'setup', ~n_R>) ]->
  [
    Out(< 'R', 'S', ~n_R >)
  ]

rule RuleStep2:
  [
    In(< 'R', 'S', n_R >),
    !Ltk('S', sk_S),
    Fr(~k_1)
  ]
  --[ Test2(), RequestRecord('S', bar(n_R)), Secret(~k_1), Honest('S'), Running('S', 'R', <'setup', n_R>) ]->
  [
    Out(< 'S', 'R', f(~k_1), sign(< f(~k_1), n_R >, sk_S) >),
    !State_S('S', ~k_1)
  ]

rule RuleStep3:
  [
    !State_S('S', k_1),
    Fr(~k_2),
    Fr(~m_1)
  ]
  --[ Test3(), Running('S', 'R', <'broadcast', k_1, ~m_1>) ]->
  [
    Out(< < ~m_1, f(~k_2) >, mac(< ~m_1, f(~k_2) >, k_1) >)
  ]

lemma executable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex #t1 #t2 #t3. Test1() @ #t1 & Test2() @ #t2 & Test3() @ #t3 & #t1 < #t2 & #t2 < #t3"

// category: secrecy
lemma secrecy_mac_key_secrecy:
  "All x #i.
     Secret(x) @ i
   ==>
       not (Ex #j. K(x) @ j)
     | (Ex X #r. LtkReveal(X) @ r & Honest(X) @ i)"

// category: injective_agreement
lemma injective_agreement_broadcast_authenticity:
  "All a b t #i.
    Commit(a, b, t) @ i
  ==>
    ( (Ex #j. Running(b, a, t) @ j
          & j < i
          & not (Ex a2 b2 #i2. Commit(a2, b2, t) @ i2
                             & not (#i2 = #i))) )
    | (Ex X #r. LtkReveal(X) @ r & Honest(X) @ i)"

// category: noninjective_agreement
lemma noninjective_agreement_setup_integrity:
  "All a b t #i.
    Commit(a, b, t) @ i
  ==>
    (Ex #j. Running(b, a, t) @ j)
    | (Ex X #r. LtkReveal(X) @ r & Honest(X) @ i)"

/*
================================================================================
SECURITY ANALYSIS SUMMARY
================================================================================

Verification Results:
---------------------
| Property                    | Category              | Verdict    |
|-----------------------------|-----------------------|------------|
| mac_key_secrecy             | secrecy               | VERIFIED   |
| broadcast_authenticity      | injective_agreement   | FALSIFIED  |
| setup_integrity             | noninjective_agreement| FALSIFIED  |

VULNERABILITY 1: Broadcast Authenticity Failure
-----------------------------------------------
Lemma: injective_agreement_broadcast_authenticity
Verdict: FALSIFIED (real counterexample)

Finding: The injective agreement property for broadcast messages does not hold.
An adversary can replay broadcast messages across different sessions, violating
the uniqueness guarantee that each Commit by a receiver should correspond to
exactly one Running by the sender.

Root Cause: The current model lacks receiver-side verification action facts
that would bind the receiver's acceptance of broadcast messages to a specific
sender session. RuleStep3 emits Running('S', 'R', ...) but there is no
corresponding Commit action on the receiver side when accepting broadcast data.
The protocol's delayed key disclosure mechanism inherently allows replay attacks
within the disclosure window.

Impact: An adversary can capture valid broadcast messages and replay them to
receivers, who cannot distinguish replayed messages from fresh ones until the
MAC key is disclosed.

VULNERABILITY 2: Setup Integrity Failure
----------------------------------------
Lemma: noninjective_agreement_setup_integrity
Verdict: FALSIFIED (real counterexample)

Finding: The non-injective agreement property for setup parameters does not
hold. A receiver's acceptance of the SetupResponse does not guarantee the
sender committed to the same f(k_1) and received the same n_R during the
same session.

Root Cause: The model has a mismatch between Commit and Running action facts:
- RuleStep1 (Receiver): Commit('R', 'S', <'setup', ~n_R>)
- RuleStep2 (Sender): Running('S', 'R', <'setup', n_R>)

The Commit is emitted before the sender processes the request, and the terms
do not match (Commit includes ~n_R as fresh, Running receives n_R as input).
This structural mismatch allows the adversary to interleave sessions and break
the agreement property.

Impact: An adversary can manipulate the setup phase to cause sender and
receiver to disagree on session parameters, potentially enabling downstream
attacks on the broadcast authentication.

VERIFIED PROPERTY: MAC Key Secrecy
----------------------------------
Lemma: secrecy_mac_key_secrecy
Verdict: VERIFIED

Finding: MAC keys remain secret from the adversary until their scheduled
disclosure time, assuming no long-term key compromise.

Reason: The Secret(~k_1) action fact in RuleStep2 correctly captures the key
generation, and the one-way function f(.) prevents the adversary from deriving
k_1 from f(k_1) before disclosure.

RECOMMENDATIONS:
----------------
1. Add receiver-side broadcast verification: Introduce a rule modeling receiver
   acceptance of broadcast messages with a Commit action fact that matches the
   sender's Running action.

2. Fix setup phase action fact alignment: Ensure Commit and Running action
   facts in the setup phase use matching term structures and are emitted at
   appropriate protocol points (Commit after verification, Running after
   processing).

3. Consider replay protection: The TESLA protocol's delayed key disclosure
   inherently permits replay within the disclosure window. Document this as an
   accepted limitation or add sequence numbers/timestamps to mitigate.
================================================================================
*/

end